<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the League of Cambrai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1508">1508</a>–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as War of the Holy League · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the League of Cambrai was fought from 1508 to 1516 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. It began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a>, Emperor Maximilian I and Ferdinand of Aragon allied against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which was routed at Agnadello in 1509; the alliances then shifted repeatedly, and by 1516 Venice had recovered most of its mainland territory.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the end of 1503 the Venetian Senate had taken Rimini, Faenza and other cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> from the dispossessed lords who offered to submit. In the spring of 1508 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> by appointing its own candidate to the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>. Julius called on Christian nations to subdue Venice, and on 10 December 1508 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a>, which provided for the partition of Venice's territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In April 1509 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and on 27 April Julius imposed an interdict on the Venetians. On 14 May Bartolomeo d'Alviano confronted the French at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello">Battle of Agnadello</a>. Outnumbered, he asked his cousin Niccolò di Pitigliano for reinforcements; Pitigliano ordered him to break off and went on his way, but Alviano fought on, and his army was surrounded and destroyed. Venice recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, which withstood a siege in September 1509.</p><p>In 1510 Julius, alarmed by the French presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> fell apart. On 4 October 1511 he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> of the Papal States, Venice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the Swiss. The French won at Ravenna on 11 April 1512 but were driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. After Julius's death Venice turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and on 23 March 1513 the two agreed at Blois to divide northern Italy between them.</p><p>The Swiss routed the French at Novara on 6 June 1513, and the English won the Battle of the Spurs on 16 August. Francis I, who had succeeded Louis XII, renewed the Venetian alliance in 1515 and won a decisive victory over the Swiss and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a> on 13 and 14 September 1515.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ferdinand II died in January 1516 and was succeeded by his grandson, the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. In August 1516 the Treaty of Noyon between Charles and Francis recognised French claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Spanish claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>Maximilian made one more attempt to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, then negotiated, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, concluded on 6 December 1516, accepted French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and confirmed Venetian claims to the remaining imperial possessions in Lombardy, except for Cremona. By January 1517 Verona had been surrendered to the Venetians, and celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ran from January to March 1517. The treaties essentially returned the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to the situation of 1508. The peace lasted only four years: the rivalry of the Valois and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as emperor in 1519 led to the Italian War of 1521-26.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the League of Cambrai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of the League of Cambrai</b>, French and Ferrarese forces under Gaston de Foix and Alfonso I d'Este defeated a Spanish-Papal army under Ramón de Cardona, Viceroy of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Schio in the Veneto, a combined Spanish and imperial army under Ramón de Cardona and Fernando d'Ávalos decisively defeated a Venetian army under Bartolomeo d'Alviano, in a significant battle of the <b>War of the League of Cambrai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-7">7 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
